{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XW4CjRYYZwe3GUzHTn6v8y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze this research project carefully to define three tiers (best/median/worst) for the Dream simulation.\n\nThe project is about VLA (Vision-Language-Action) token pruning, targeting CoRL 2026. The core finding is a 2-axis framework: connector architecture (token coupling) × task complexity determines pruning sensitivity.\n\nLet me understand the current state:\n\n**What's confirmed:**\n1. 2-Axis framework (Claim 1): Token coupling (connector) × Task complexity\n2. Pixel-shuffle catastrophic (Claim 2): SmolVLA 0% at k=0.95\n\n\n3. Linear projection behavior varies by architecture (Claim 3a): π0 and π0.5 handle pruning well, while X-VLA shows threshold-dependent degradation\n4. MLP projectors degrade smoothly across pruning levels (Claim 3b): OpenVLA demonstrates this pattern\n5. Coefficient of variation predicts optimal pruning (Claim 5): Scaling law holds at k=0.5 and k=0.7\n6. VTR recovers pixel-shuffle failures (Claim 4): Restores performance from 0% to ~59%, matching baseline with flat M-curve\n\n**What's pending/uncertain:**\n- π0.5 cross-suite data (Goal\n\n/Long) remains incomplete despite partial collection\n- VTR failed on MLP projectors with 0% success rate due to overfitting\n- OFT evaluation blocked by GPU incompatibility on Blackwell hardware\n- Multi-seed confirmations still in progress\n- Attention-based pruning for X-VLA showing promise with new E5 results\n- OpenVLA cross-suite evaluation (Goal, Long, Spatial) partially collected\n\n**Key risks:**\nThe 2-axis framework might not survive when all cross-suite data arrives, since the original 4-class taxonomy already showed cracks. VTR only works for pixel-shuffle, not MLP projectors, which limits the method's generality. The CV scaling law has edge cases like the k=0.3 reversal for π0.5. Everything's simulation-only without real robot validation, and several experiments are still running.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the scenarios for how this could play out. In the best case, pending data confirms the 2-axis framework holds across all suites, the scaling law stays consistent, and the attention pruning results give us another strong contribution—that would make for a tight story. More realistically, I'll probably see some noise in the cross-suite results and need to add caveats around the framework's exceptions. Worst case, the cross-suite data fundamentally breaks the 2-axis framework and the CV prediction fails entirely.\n\nTo track how things are actually going, I'm defining specific metrics: the interaction strength between the two axes, whether the scaling law holds monotonically across conditions, whether the L2norm advantage pattern repeats across different task types, whether VTR generalizes beyond pixel-shuffle, and whether the overall framework explains the data without major exceptions.\n\nNow looking at what's actually confirmed versus what's still uncertain: SmolVLA shows a clean cliff at k=0.95 across all suites, OpenVLA has a smooth graded decline from 74% down to 20.6%, and X-VLA shows a substantial 27.8 percentage point gap between Object and Goal tasks at k=0.9. The CV scaling law is monotonic at k=0.5, and VTR compression on SmolVLA stays nearly lossless. But the cross-suite pattern for π0.5 and Goal/Long tasks is only partially confirmed—some results show the L2norm advantage narrowing or disappearing.\n\nActually, digging into the π0.5 data more carefully: Goal tasks show L2norm at 90.6% versus random at 62.2% at k=0.9, and Long tasks show L2norm at 78.4% versus random at 45.2% at k=0.85, both strongly favoring L2norm and matching the Object pattern. The random pruning still exhibits that dramatic cliff behavior. The attention-based pruning results for X-VLA are particularly strong—k=0.5 reaches 99.2% on Object and 90.2% on Long—though I'm still uncertain whether this generalizes beyond X-VLA, especially since the Goal task initially showed 0% before jumping to 98.2%.\n\nOpenVLA's cross-suite coverage is incomplete with only Object fully evaluated, while Goal and Long baselines sit at 74.8% and 52.8% respectively, and VTR failed on non-SmolVLA models. Now I'm structuring the three outcome tiers: the best case requires all five claims to hold cleanly with the 2-axis framework explaining everything, monotonic CV scaling across all conditions, and E5's attention pruning as a strong sixth finding, with VTR's connector-specificity reframed as a feature. The key metrics for this tier would be Object-to-Goal gaps of at least 25 percentage points for token-entangling connectors but 5 or fewer for token-independent ones, plus confirmed monotonic scaling at both k=0.5 and k=0.7.\n\nThe median scenario has the framework directionally correct but with several exceptions—the k=0.3 reversal complicates the CV scaling story, some cross-suite results need post-hoc explanation, and VTR only succeeds with one connector type, forcing me to soften claims about predicting optimal strategies to just moderate compression. The worst case reveals that the task complexity axis isn't as clean as hoped, requiring too many qualifications to the framework, with CV prediction failing across some model-suite combinations, though the paper could still reach CoRL if reframed as empirical observations rather than a principled framework. Looking at the data more carefully now, I'm noticing that most of the evidence actually points toward something stronger than the worst case.\n\nThe real risks are the pending experiments that might show inconsistencies, reviewers dismissing the simulation-only limitation, VTR being too narrow since it only works for SmolVLA, and the E5 attention pruning results potentially contradicting my \"pruning fails on entangling connectors\" narrative. Actually, the E5 results are doing the opposite—attention-based pruning achieves strong performance on X-VLA even at high compression, which actually validates the framework by showing that the right importance metric can handle entangling connectors. This strengthens the method contribution significantly. Though I need to verify the Goal task results since E5 showed 0% initially then jumped to 98.2% after a fix.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the outcome scenarios. In the best case, all the cross-suite π0.5 data confirms L2norm's advantage for token-independent connectors, E5's attention pruning gives me a clean story about how importance metrics matter, the 2-axis framework holds with no major exceptions, and VTR's connector-dependency becomes a feature showing how understanding the problem leads to better methods. In a more median scenario, the framework holds for about 80% of conditions with a few edge cases, E5 attention pruning only partially succeeds across different suites, π0.5 shows some inconsistencies like the k=0.3 reversal and non-monotonicity, and I'd need to qualify my claims about CV as a predictor of base sensitivity.\n\nThe worst case is that new cross-suite data reveals the 2-axis framework is too simplistic, task complexity doesn't cleanly separate with some \"simple\" tasks behaving like \"complex\" ones, the CV scaling law breaks for new models or conditions, and VTR's failure on OpenVLA undermines the method contribution entirely. In that scenario, I'd need to reframe the whole paper as an empirical study of VLA token pruning rather than a principled framework.\n\nNow I'm identifying the key metrics to track, starting with the axis-2 amplification ratio that measures how much more entangling connectors drop performance on complex versus simple tasks at the same k value—currently seeing about a 17x difference between X-VLA Goal and Object, which I'd want to be at least 10x in the best case.\n\nNext I'm looking at the CV-strategy correlation using Spearman's coefficient to see if higher coefficient of variation consistently predicts which strategy works better across models, and then the VTR recovery rate for SmolVLA to measure how well the pruning method preserves baseline performance.\n\nFor cross-suite consistency, I need to check what fraction of model-suite-k combinations actually match my framework's predictions, and then compare the success rates at k=0.5 across the token-independent and token-entangling approaches to see the headline difference.\n\nThe main uncertainty now is whether reviewers will accept the 2-axis framework as a compelling way to organize these results. or see it as post-hoc?\n2. **Method contribution weakness**: VTR only for SmolVLA, E5 attention pruning is new and might not be ready\n3. **Missing data**: Some cross-suite combinations still pending\n4. **Competitor risk**: 4 concurrent works could scoop parts\n\nLet me redefine the tiers with this in mind:\n\nActually, I think the tiers should be about what happens when we try to write the paper and present it, given the current data state and what might still change in 4-7 days.\n\nLet me focus on what's actually uncertain:\n\n1. π0.5 Goal/Long remaining experiments - will they confirm the pattern?\n2. OpenVLA cross-suite (Goal/Long) - very sparse data, might show unexpected patterns\n3. E5 attention pruning - will this add or complicate the story?\n4. Whether the uniformly critical finding for π0.5 generalizes across different suites\n5. VTR on non-SmolVLA - already failed, but VTC-only experiments still pending\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the YAML structure. I'll track the key metrics that matter most: the amplification effect on complex versus simple tasks for entangling connectors, the correlation between cross-validation strategy and performance deltas, how consistently the framework predictions hold across different model-suite-k combinations, the recovery ratio for VTR on SmolVLA, and my estimate of reviewer acceptance probability given all the evidence.\n\nI'm now defining specific metric ranges where the best case shows strong cross-suite validation and high negative correlation for the CV strategy, while the worst case reflects incomplete data and potential contradictions. I need to be careful about the direction of these metrics—for Spearman correlation, the absolute value matters since negative correlations can still be strong, so I should compare |ρ| across tiers. Cross-suite validation and axis amplification both improve with higher values, and I'm thinking through what each performance tier actually represents: the best case shows a clean, principled framework with solid cross-suite validation and all claims holding up, while the median tier shows directional support but with notable edge cases that need qualification.\n\nThe worst case reveals the framework oversimplifies the actual behavior—some models show inconsistencies across suites that the framework doesn't predict, CV fails as a universal predictor, and the paper needs reframing as empirical observations rather than mechanistic understanding, though it's still publishable at CoRL with weaker claims.\n\nNow I'm writing out the final YAML configuration.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":104600,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":104600},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3b80564-13c6-4910-954a-7ecb90e1c43c","uuid":"55b65be3-61a6-4ee5-8653-86e19aebc572"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XW4CjRYYZwe3GUzHTn6v8y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"2-axis framework cleanly validated across all model×suite combinations; CV diagnostic and scaling law hold without major exceptions; E5 attention pruning adds complementary method contribution\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Axis-2 interaction (task complexity amplification) replicates for π0.5 Goal/Long with pattern matching X-VLA: entangling connectors show ≥20pp amplification, independent connectors show ≤10pp\"\n      - \"CV-strategy scaling law (Δ(R-L2) monotonically decreasing with CV) holds at k=0.5 AND k=0.7 across all 3 tested models — already confirmed, no new exceptions emerge from remaining seeds\"\n      - \"π0.5 'uniformly critical' behavior (random cliff at k=0.85-0.925, L2norm plateau) generalizes to Spatial/Goal with consistent mechanism (concentrated vs distributed critical tokens)\"\n      - \"VTR connector-dependency (works for pixel-shuffle, fails for MLP) is a clean, explainable result that strengthens rather than weakens the framework\"\n      - \"E5 attention-based pruning for X-VLA (Object k=0.5=99.2%, Long k=0.5=90.2%) generalizes to Goal suite, providing a 'correct importance metric rescues entangling connectors' story\"\n    number_ranges:\n      axis2_amplification_pp: [25, 45]  # entangling connector SR_drop(complex) - SR_drop(simple) at same k\n      cv_strategy_abs_spearman: [0.85, 1.0]  # |ρ| between CV and Δ(R-L2) at k=0.5\n      cross_suite_framework_accuracy_pct: [90, 100]  # % of model×suite×k combos where prediction matches\n      claims_surviving_review: [4, 5]  # out of 5 core claims\n      vtr_smolvla_recovery_ratio: [0.95, 1.05]  # VTR_SR / baseline_SR, already confirmed ~1.0\n      pi05_l2norm_random_gap_k05_pp: [30, 40]  # across suites, L2norm consistently worse\n    narrative_direction: \"原始 framing 完全成立。Paper title 保持不变。5 claims 全部保留。2-axis framework 是 principled contribution（非 post-hoc observation）。CV diagnostic + VTR method 形成完整的 Diagnose-then-Compress pipeline。Analysis 是 primary contribution，method 是 well-motivated secondary。E5 attention pruning 作为 §4.7 或 supplementary 增加实用价值。Reviewer concern RC7（仅一个 benchmark）被 4 suites 完全化解。\"\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"2-axis framework directionally correct but有 2-3 个 edge cases 需要 qualify；CV scaling law 在 moderate compression 成立但在 extreme k 有 reversal；method contribution 限于 SmolVLA；需要调整若干 claim 强度\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Axis-2 interaction 对 X-VLA 成立（27.8pp gap confirmed）但 π0.5 Goal/Long 的 amplification 弱于预期（<15pp），因为 token-independent connector 的 task sensitivity 本身较低\"\n      - \"CV scaling law 在 k=0.5 成立，但 k=0.3 出现 reversal（π0.5 L2norm wins +14.6pp）且 k=0.7 的 gap 明显小于 k=0.5，说明 CV 只在特定 compression regime 有效\"\n      - \"π0.5 random cliff (k=0.85-0.925) 是 Object-specific 而非 universal：Spatial 没有 catastrophic dip（已见 58.4% floor vs Object 5%），Goal/Long 的 cliff profile 不同\"\n      - \"VTR 仅对 pixel-shuffle 有效，OpenVLA VTR 已确认 0% SR。VTC-only M=32/64 training 如果也失败，method contribution 进一步缩窄\"\n      - \"E5 attention pruning 在 Object/Spatial/Long 表现好但 Goal 初始为 0%（后修复为 98.2%），结果可靠性需要额外 seed 确认\"\n    number_ranges:\n      axis2_amplification_pp: [10, 25]\n      cv_strategy_abs_spearman: [0.6, 0.85]\n      cross_suite_framework_accuracy_pct: [75, 90]\n      claims_surviving_review: [3, 4]\n      vtr_smolvla_recovery_ratio: [0.90, 1.0]\n      pi05_l2norm_random_gap_k05_pp: [20, 35]  # 可能 Spatial/Goal gap 小于 Object 的 36pp\n    narrative_direction: \"需要调整 claims 强度。Claim 1 从 'governed by two axes' 弱化为 'primarily explained by connector architecture, with task complexity as a modulating factor'。Claim 5 加限定条件 'at moderate compression (k=0.5-0.7)'，k=0.3 reversal 移入 Discussion。VTR 明确定位为 pixel-shuffle-specific solution 而非 universal method。Paper identity 从 'analysis + method' 调整为 'primarily analysis with targeted method for worst-case architecture'。Title 可能改为更保守的 framing，如 'How Vision Connectors Shape Token Pruning Robustness in VLAs'。仍提交 CoRL，但 contribution statement 更聚焦于 diagnostic insight 而非 complete framework。\"\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"2-axis framework 过度简化——新的 cross-suite 数据揭示 task complexity 轴不独立于 connector 轴；CV 作为 universal predictor 失败；paper 需要 reframe 为 empirical study 但仍冲 CoRL\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"π0.5 cross-suite 数据显示 token-independent connector 在 Goal/Long 上也出现 >20pp amplification（实际已见 random k=0.9 Goal=62.2% vs Object=9% 的 anomalous pattern），破坏 '独立 connector 不受 task complexity 影响' 的假设\"\n      - \"CV scaling law 在 cross-suite 条件下失效：同一 CV 的 model 在不同 suite 上的 Δ(R-L2) 差异 >15pp，说明 CV 只在 within-suite 有效，不是 architecture-level property\"\n      - \"E5 attention pruning 证明 X-VLA 的 'entangling connector 不可剪' 叙事有误——用 attention 替代 L2norm 即可在 k=0.5 保持 99%+ SR，这意味着问题不在 connector entanglement 而在 importance metric 选择\"\n      - \"VTR 完全是 SmolVLA-specific，且 M=96 dip (p=0.034) 暗示 VTR 训练不稳定\"\n      - \"Reviewer 可能指出：如果 attention pruning 解决了 X-VLA 的问题，那么 2-axis framework 的 Axis-1 定义有根本缺陷——'token entanglement' 不是关键因素，importance metric 才是\"\n    number_ranges:\n      axis2_amplification_pp: [3, 15]  # token-independent connectors 也显示 amplification，削弱 interaction 的 clean separation\n      cv_strategy_abs_spearman: [0.3, 0.6]  # cross-suite variance 使 correlation 显著减弱\n      cross_suite_framework_accuracy_pct: [50, 75]  # 多个 model×suite 组合 framework 预测错误\n      claims_surviving_review: [2, 3]\n      vtr_smolvla_recovery_ratio: [0.85, 0.95]  # VTR 稳定性存疑\n      pi05_l2norm_random_gap_k05_pp: [10, 25]  # cross-suite 差异大，Object 36pp 但 Goal 可能 <15pp\n    narrative_direction: \"核心 reframe：从 'principled 2-axis framework' 转为 'comprehensive empirical study of VLA token pruning across architectures and tasks'。不再声称发现了 governing principle，而是提供了 community 需要的系统性 benchmark + surprising findings catalog。新 angle：(1) 'importance metric selection matters more than connector architecture' — L2norm 在低 CV model 上 actively harmful 是独立的 novel finding，不需要 2-axis framework 支撑；(2) 'attention-based pruning universally outperforms norm-based pruning for VLAs' 如果 E5 数据可靠；(3) VTR 作为 case study 而非 general method。Title 改为 'A Systematic Study of Token Pruning Strategies Across VLA Architectures'。仍然 submit CoRL（不降级），因为 5 models × 4 suites × systematic comparison 本身就是 substantial empirical contribution，即使没有 unified theory。Claims 缩减为 3 个 empirical observations + 1 个 practical guideline。\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":104600,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":104600},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3b80564-13c6-4910-954a-7ecb90e1c43c","uuid":"515ba42f-360d-4055-a99e-cdf3f8ee069f"}
